Error contract
-------------

All malformed input and bad arguments are reported by throwing a GifError.
GifError extends the built-in Error (so `instanceof Error` and existing
try/catch blocks keep working) and adds two properties:

  .name    always "GifError"
  .stage   short identifier for the parse/encode stage that failed
  .offset  byte offset inside the buffer where the problem was detected,
           or null for pure argument validation that has no buffer position

Decoder stages (thrown by GifReader, including decodeAndBlitFrameRGBA /
decodeAndBlitFrameBGRA):

  header, logical-screen, global-color-table, block,
  application-extension, graphics-control-extension, comment-extension,
  image-descriptor, local-color-table, image-data, lzw-min-code-size,
  lzw-subblock, lzw-data, lzw-code, lzw-output, trailer, frame-rect,
  palette-index, output-buffer

The reader never treats missing bytes as zero: every read is bounds-checked,
so a truncated file fails with the stage and offset of the first unreadable
byte instead of silently decoding garbage.  Frames must lie inside the
logical canvas, transparent and pixel indices must resolve in the frame's
color table, and LZW streams are rejected for early termination, illegal
dictionary references, and pixel underflow/overflow.  A destination pixel
buffer that is too short raises an output-buffer error before any pixel is
written, leaving its existing contents untouched.

Writer stages (thrown by GifWriter):

  writer-arguments  non-integer or out-of-range dimensions, coordinates,
                    delay, disposal, transparent/palette indices, palette
                    colors, or a frame rectangle outside the canvas.  These
                    carry a null offset and change no encoder state.
  writer-buffer     the fixed output buffer ran out of space; .offset is the
                    position that could not be written.

A failed addFrame is atomic: it restores the output position, the bytes that
existed before the call and the ended flag, then rethrows.  After catching a
writer-buffer error you can swap in a larger buffer with setOutputBuffer()
(or use setOutputBufferPosition()) and continue adding legal frames to the
same encoder.  A failed frame added after end() never removes the trailer.

The original CommonJS exports are unchanged, with GifError added alongside:

  exports.GifWriter, exports.GifReader, exports.GifError

Plain Arrays, Uint8Arrays and Node Buffers all remain supported as output
buffers.


Running the tests
-----------------

The regression suite has no third-party dependencies:

  node test/regression.js

It exits with status 0 only when every test passes; otherwise it prints each
failed test by name with its stage/offset error and exits non-zero.  The
suite builds its GIF fixtures with its own LZW encoder and byte builder
(separate code from omggif.js), checks decoded pixels against hand-written
expectations, sweeps every byte-length truncation of each valid sample,
exercises exact-capacity and one-byte-short buffers, and verifies recovery
after failed and post-end frames.

